<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Garden – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Our Garden.</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> A Tall Tree!</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Catch the main idea of a short, simple story or announcement with visual support or illustrations. Interactive: Respond to simple conversations on familiar topics by using gestures or by pointing.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize nature-related words like "tree," "flowers," and "bushes" in simple conversations or stories (e.g., "The tree is tall." "The flowers are pink."). Can follow basic instructions related to outdoor environments in conversations (e.g., "Look at the tall tree."). Can respond to questions about nature by pointing or using gestures (e.g., pointing to a tree in response to the question "Do you see a tree?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage activates prior knowledge, introduces the theme, and models new language with clear pronunciation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Garden Tour" using a big picture poster of a garden. Students listen to the teacher say names of items in the garden and point to them. "Look! It's a garden!" (point to poster). "Do you see a tree?" (point to a tree). "Yes, a tree!" (choral repetition). Introduce a simple song like "The Garden Song" (e.g., "A garden, a garden, green and bright! Tall trees, small flowers, what a sight!"). Students listen and do simple TPR actions like swaying for a tree or wiggling fingers for flowers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of "garden," "tree," and "flower." Teacher says each word clearly multiple times, pointing to the word and the image. "Listen: garden. /g/ garden." (Emphasize /g/ sound). "Listen: tree. /t/ tree." (Emphasize /t/ sound). Students listen and repeat chorally. The teacher then uses short sentences: "This is a tall tree." (points to a tall tree picture/flashcard). "The flowers are pink." (points to pink flowers). Students listen attentively, teacher encourages pointing and gestures to show understanding.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a picture of a house vs. a garden) "Is this a house or a garden?" (Students point to the garden picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a tree and a flower) "What is this?" (pointing to the tree) or "Is this a tree?" (Students nod/shake head or point to the tree).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Introduction of new language through authentic input, focusing on general comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher presents a short, simple audio story titled "My Garden." The story will feature a child describing their garden. "In my garden, I see a tall tree. The leaves are green. I see pink flowers. I like my garden." The teacher shows large, colorful picture cards or a simple storybook with illustrations matching the audio as students listen.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short narrative excerpt (audio with visual storybook/large picture cards). The genre is a descriptive narrative, informal register, presented as an audio recording with visual support.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Catch the main idea of a short, simple story or announcement with visual support or illustrations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the story about? A house or a garden?" (Students listen and point to a picture of a garden).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (using visuals):</b> Teacher plays the audio sentence by sentence or short phrases. After each, the teacher shows two contrasting flashcards (e.g., a tall tree and a short bush). "Listen: 'I see a tall tree.' Is this a tall tree? (points to the tall tree flashcard). Yes/No?" (Students nod for yes, shake for no, or use thumbs up/down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Pointing):</b> "Listen to the story again. When you hear 'pink flowers', point to the pink flowers picture." "When you hear 'green leaves', point to the green leaves." (Focus on target vocabulary: garden, tree, flower, leaf, pink, green, tall).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Activities to reinforce accuracy of newly acquired language through controlled and semi-controlled tasks, mainly focusing on listening comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Listening.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Noun + verb + adjective (e.g., "The flowers are pink."), Present simple (e.g., "This is a tall tree.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> garden, tree, flower, plant, leaf, bush, pink, green, tall, what, is, are.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /g/ in "garden," /t/ in "tree," /s/ in "sun."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Point (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher lays out flashcards of "garden," "tree," "flower," "leaf," "bush," "sun" on a table. The teacher says a word (e.g., "tree"). Students listen and point to the correct flashcard. Repeat for all target vocabulary. Then, the teacher says simple sentences: "This is a tall tree." "The flowers are pink." "The sun is yellow." Students listen and point to the corresponding pictures. Emphasize /g/, /t/, /s/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Identify (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher shows students a worksheet with multiple pictures (e.g., three pictures: a tall tree, a short bush, pink flowers). The teacher says a sentence, e.g., "Look! This is a tall tree." Students listen and circle or mark the correct picture on their worksheet. Repeat with "The flowers are pink," "The bush is green," "My garden has a big tree."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Follow the Instruction (Interactive Listening):</b> Students work in pairs with a shared picture of a garden. The teacher gives instructions to the whole class: "Student A, listen: point to the tree. Student B, listen: point to the flowers." Then, students switch roles or the teacher asks "What color is the flower?" and students point to the flower and gesture its color, reinforcing receptive listening and simple interactive response. The teacher circulates, observing students' listening comprehension and pointing accuracy.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the language actively in a realistic communicative task, focusing on fluency and task completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Our Imaginary Garden Walk"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are divided into small groups. Each group receives a large picture of a garden (or a blank paper to draw their own simple garden elements as the teacher speaks). The teacher acts as a "tour guide" for an imaginary garden walk. The teacher describes the garden, and students listen and point to the elements or draw them on their paper.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher starts: "Welcome to our garden! It's a big, beautiful garden."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Look! There is a tall tree. Point to the tall tree!" (Students listen and point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"And here are the flowers. What color are the flowers? Yes, the flowers are pink." (Students listen and point to pink flowers).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you see the green leaves on the tree? Point to the green leaves."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is this? This is a little plant." (Students listen and point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I like my garden. It's so nice." (Sociolinguistic: Respect for nature).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in understanding spoken English, collaborative interaction (pointing together in groups), and communicative task completion (successfully identifying described garden elements through listening). Teacher uses WH-questions like "What color is the flower?" and waits for students to point or gesture an answer.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Evaluation of learning through formative assessment and initial reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Observation Checklist. The teacher observes students' ability to follow instructions and identify vocabulary by pointing during the "Imaginary Garden Walk."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a simple worksheet with a picture of a garden containing a tree, flowers, and a bush. The teacher reads out descriptions: "Circle the tall tree," "Color the pink flowers," "Put an X on the green bush." Students listen and perform the actions on their worksheet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Listening focus):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Receptive Accuracy:</b> Correctly identifies and points to objects based on auditory input (e.g., "tree," "flowers," "bush").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Instruction Following:</b> Accurately follows simple, one-step instructions (e.g., "Circle," "Color").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Recognition:</b> Demonstrates recognition of nature-related vocabulary (e.g., "tall," "pink," "green").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students draw their favorite thing they heard about in the garden (e.g., a "tall tree" or "pink flowers") on a small paper slip and show it to the teacher as they leave. The teacher asks, "What did you draw?" or "Do you like the tree?"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson and planning for future learning based on student and teacher insights.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you hear today?" (Teacher shows flashcards, students point to the words they heard).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you like our garden story? Give a thumbs up or down."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in engaging students and developing listening comprehension at Pre-A1.3 level? (e.g., TPR, visual aids, short sentences, clear pronunciation of /g/, /t/, /s/ sounds).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson, especially for students who struggled with listening to specific vocabulary or instructions? (e.g., more repetition, smaller groups, one-on-one checks, simpler sentences).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will engage with a simple text/picture story using the same garden-related vocabulary (tree, flower, garden, plant, leaf, pink, green, tall) to develop visual recognition of the words they have primarily heard today. The focus will shift to matching spoken words to written words and understanding simple sentences in written form, building on the auditory recognition from this lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>